INT. DIALYSIS UNIT - NIGHT

A long, low room of reclining chairs and blinking machines. Most of the beds are empty. A rain-streaked window frames the parking lot. The machines hum a single note, patient as insects.

DR. HELENE MARCHETTI (50s, grey at the temples, scrubs worn soft with washing) pulls a stool beside the last occupied chair. ROSALIND AKINS (70s, shawl, a library paperback open on her lap) does not look up from her page. Blood moves slow and dark through the tubing between her arm and the machine.

She turns a page. She is not reading it.

HELENE
How's the pressure?

ROSALIND
You tell me.

Helene checks the monitor. Taps the screen. Writes nothing.

HELENE
Good enough.

ROSALIND
Mm.

The machine ticks. Helene folds her hands in her lap, unfolds them. She looks at the paperback.

HELENE
Is it any good?

ROSALIND
Third time through.

HELENE
That's a yes, then.

Rosalind closes the book on her finger. She looks, at last, at the doctor.

ROSALIND
You've got your father's habit.

HELENE
Which one?

ROSALIND
Asking the thing next to the thing.

Helene does not answer. A nurse's cart rattles somewhere beyond the glass. In the pause, both of them reach for the call button at the same moment, hands touching on the plastic. They pull back.

HELENE
I'll get it.
ROSALIND
^I'll get it.

Neither moves. The machine counts down another minute. Rosalind opens the book again, and Helene, after a moment, takes it gently from her hands, finds the place, and begins to read aloud, badly, in a low voice, to no one in particular.
